00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:03.679 align:start position:0%
the morning after a politically stormy  night  it took 50 mps to vote against and seal  the fate of the pro-serbian and  pro-russian ruling coalition led by  drita nabazovic discontent over the  signing of a long disputed accord with a  powerful serbian orthodox church the spc  as well as stagnation over the country's  path towards integration with the  european union led the majority in  parliament to withdraw their support for  obazovich's leadership obazovic came to  office in april and has presided over  the shortest period in power in the  country's political history marking a  new era of instability he had attempted  to fend off the vote of no confidence by  offering a cabinet reshuffle  to no avail  i'm happy and i congratulate the new  coalition of takanovich and becket  i hope that they will quickly form a  government we are ready to step down  it was the signing of the accord with  the spc that sealed abbasovich's  political fate it reignited tensions and  was fiercely criticized by the president  milo jukanovic  a third of montenegro's population  identify as serbs and the largest  religious group serbian orthodoxy  while the country broke away from serbia  in 2006 the spc never fully recognized  its independence  critics of the accord including the  president say the church is harboring  the interests of serbia rather than  montenegro  serbia rules the axis of the sea and  russia wants also next to the adriatic  so i think that they are trying to use  this destabilize montenegro in order to  make weaker also  nato alliance and montenegro is the  weakest element of the of the of the  nato  president giulkanovich's most urgent  task is to appoint a new prime minister  who will have 30 days to form a cabinet  even then it may prove too weak to forge  your way out of the crisis with many  calling for early elections to resolve  this political dilemma sony gallego al  jazeera
 

